@footy_lunatic I don't disagree that some people involved in the protests committed violence, and those actions should be condemned. But that doesn't erase what happened to the wider Lhotshampa population. The historical record documents discriminatory citizenship policies, the 1988 census, restrictions on Nepali language and culture, mass arrests, abuse, and the forced displacement of tens of thousands of Nepali-speaking Bhutanese in the early 1990s. Human Rights Watch also documents that some people were forced to sign 'voluntary migration' forms before leaving.
And bringing up the 2023 fake Bhutanese refugee scam doesn't change the history of what happened in the 1990s. Tek Nath Rizal's arrest was a separate case decades later. We can condemn the actions of individuals without using those actions to justify collective punishment of an entire ethnic community.
I'm glad that Bhutanese people today can practice their culture and religion more freely. That's a positive thing. But acknowledging today's situation doesn't require erasing or denying the experiences of the people who were expelled.
